train a Scikit-learn machine learning model in Amazon SageMaker, using training data stored in Amazon S3.

Overall Workflow

Your Training Data
       │
       ▼
S3 Bucket
insurance-prediction-bucket
       │
       ▼
Amazon SageMaker Training Job
       │
       ▼
train.py executes
       │
       ▼
ML Model Created
       │
       ▼
Model Saved to S3

====

from sagemaker.sklearn.estimator import SKLearn
from sagemaker import get_execution_role
import sagemaker
from utils import append_to_file
session = sagemaker.Session()
role = get_execution_role()
bucket = 'insurance-prediction-bucket'  # Your custom S3 bucket
prefix = 'insurance-data'
sklearn_estimator = SKLearn(
    entry_point="train.py",
    role=role,
    instance_type="ml.m5.large",  # or "ml.t2.medium" for free tier
    framework_version="1.2-1",
    py_version="py3",
    output_path=f"s3://{bucket}/{prefix}/output",
    sagemaker_session=session
)
append_to_file("output.txt","framework_version 1.2-1")
# Start training
sklearn_estimator.fit({"train": f"s3://insurance-prediction-bucket/insurance-data/"})

image_uri=sklearn_estimator.training_image_uri()

append_to_file("output.txt","Image URI",image_uri)

model_s3_uri = sklearn_estimator.model_data

append_to_file("output.txt","model_s3_uri",model_s3_uri)

print("Model S3 URI:", model_s3_uri)



====

Simple Meaning of the Entire Code

Take insurance data from Amazon S3 → use SageMaker to run train.py → train a Scikit-learn model → save the trained model back to Amazon S3 → get the model's S3 location.

=====


| Code                                              | Explanation                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `from sagemaker.sklearn.estimator import SKLearn` | Imports the SageMaker Scikit-learn estimator                       |
| `from sagemaker import get_execution_role`        | Gets the IAM role used by SageMaker                                |
| `import sagemaker`                                | Imports the SageMaker Python SDK                                   |
| `from utils import append_to_file`                | Imports your custom function to save information into `output.txt` |
| `session = sagemaker.Session()`                   | Creates a SageMaker session to communicate with AWS services       |
| `role = get_execution_role()`                     | Gets the IAM execution role and its permissions                    |
| `bucket = 'insurance-prediction-bucket'`          | Your S3 bucket where data and model files are stored               |
| `prefix = 'insurance-data'`                       | Defines the folder/path inside the S3 bucket                       |


====

1. Creating the SageMaker Scikit-learn Estimator

----

sklearn_estimator = SKLearn(
    entry_point="train.py",
    role=role,
    instance_type="ml.m5.large",
    framework_version="1.2-1",
    py_version="py3",
    output_path=f"s3://{bucket}/{prefix}/output",
    sagemaker_session=session
)

----
This tells SageMaker:

| Parameter                     | Meaning                                              |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `entry_point="train.py"`      | Your ML training code                                |
| `role=role`                   | IAM permissions SageMaker will use                   |
| `instance_type="ml.m5.large"` | EC2 compute instance used for training               |
| `framework_version="1.2-1"`   | Scikit-learn framework version                       |
| `py_version="py3"`            | Python 3 environment                                 |
| `output_path`                 | Location where the trained model will be saved in S3 |
| `sagemaker_session`           | Connects the estimator with your SageMaker session   |


====

Output location
s3://insurance-prediction-bucket/insurance-data/output

After training, your model artifacts will be stored in this S3 location.

====

2. Saving Framework Information
append_to_file("output.txt", "framework_version 1.2-1")

This writes the framework version information into:

output.txt

This is likely used to keep a record of your training process.

====

3. Starting the Training Job
sklearn_estimator.fit(
    {"train": "s3://insurance-prediction-bucket/insurance-data/"}
)

This is the most important part.

It tells SageMaker:

Take the training data from this S3 location and run train.py using the specified Scikit-learn environment.

=====

Flow
S3
insurance-prediction-bucket
        │
        └── insurance-data/
                │
                ▼
             train.py
                │
                ▼
        SageMaker Training
                │
                ▼
          Trained Model

=====

4. Getting the Docker Training Image URI
image_uri = sklearn_estimator.training_image_uri()

SageMaker uses a prebuilt Docker container image containing:

Python
Scikit-learn
Required SageMaker training environment

The image_uri gives the location/identifier of that container image.

Then:

append_to_file("output.txt", "Image URI", image_uri)

saves the image information to your output.txt file.

=====

5. Getting the Trained Model Location
model_s3_uri = sklearn_estimator.model_data

After training is complete, this gets the S3 URI of the trained model.

For example, it may look similar to:

s3://insurance-prediction-bucket/insurance-data/output/...

Then:

print("Model S3 URI:", model_s3_uri)

displays the exact location.

====

mportant IAM Connection 🔐

This line:

role = get_execution_role()

gets the SageMaker Execution Role.

That role needs permissions to access your S3 bucket, which is why you created an IAM policy with permissions such as:

s3:PutObject
s3:GetObject
s3:ListBucket

So your complete architecture is:

        IAM Role
            │
            │ Gives Permission
            ▼
Amazon SageMaker ──────────► Amazon S3
       │                         │
       │ Read Training Data      │
       │ ◄───────────────────────┤
       │
       │ Train train.py
       ▼
 Trained ML Model
       │
       │ Upload Model
       ▼
Amazon S3 Output Folder

This is a typical Amazon SageMaker ML training workflow.

